---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const email = 'kaleem@holisticresilience.org';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What Write-Ahead is, and who's writing it.">
  <a class="back-link" href="/">← the book</a>
  <h1 style="margin-top:14px;">About</h1>

  <section class="author-card" data-sketch-frame>
    <div class="monogram" data-sketch-frame="circle" data-sketch-stroke="--accent">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 120 120" role="img" aria-label="A hand-drawn database cylinder">
        <ellipse cx="60" cy="34" rx="34" ry="12" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent)" stroke-width="3"/>
        <path d="M26 34 V86 C26 92.6 41.2 98 60 98 C78.8 98 94 92.6 94 86 V34"
              fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
        <path d="M26 56 C26 62.6 41.2 68 60 68 C78.8 68 94 62.6 94 56"
              fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-soft)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
        <path d="M26 72 C26 78.6 41.2 84 60 84 C78.8 84 94 78.6 94 72"
              fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-soft)" stroke-width="2.2"/>
        <circle cx="46" cy="33" r="3" fill="var(--accent2)"/>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <div class="author-text">
      <h2 class="author-name">Write-Ahead</h2>
      <p class="author-role">database internals · one chapter at a time</p>
      <p>
        I kept using databases without really knowing what happened after I pressed
        <code>Enter</code> on a query. So I started taking the engine apart — the page
        format, the B-tree, the write-ahead log, the lock manager — and drawing each piece
        until it made sense.
      </p>
      <p>
        Write-Ahead is that teardown, written in public. The bet is simple: if I can
        <em>draw</em> how a part works, I actually understand it — and so will you.
      </p>
      <p class="contact">
        Say hi · <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <h2>What this is</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    <b>Write-Ahead</b> is a hand-drawn tour of how a database works on the inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every chapter takes one mechanism the docs gloss over and rebuilds it from first
    principles — with sketches, byte layouts, and small animations you can poke at. Less
    "here's the API," more "here's what the bytes are doing on disk." The goal is that you
    leave able to <em>picture</em> the engine, not just call it.
  </p>

  <style>
    .author-card {
      position: relative;
      display: flex;
      gap: 26px;
      align-items: flex-start;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      background: var(--panel);
      border-radius: 12px;
      padding: 24px 24px 20px;
      margin: 1.6em 0 2.4em;
      box-shadow: 0 12px 30px -22px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
    }
    .monogram {
      position: relative;
      flex: none;
      padding: 11px;
      border-radius: 50%;
    }
    .monogram svg {
      width: 150px;
      height: 150px;
      display: block;
      background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 8%, var(--panel));
      border-radius: 50%;
    }
    .author-text { flex: 1 1 260px; min-width: 0; }
    .author-name {
      font-family: var(--font-display);
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 2.6rem);
      margin: 0 0 2px;
      line-height: 1;
    }
    .author-role {
      font-family: var(--font-hand);
      color: var(--accent);
      font-size: 1.1rem;
      margin: 0 0 12px;
    }
    .author-text p { margin: 0 0 0.9em; }
    .contact {
      font-family: var(--font-hand);
      color: var(--ink-soft);
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }
    .contact a { color: var(--accent); text-underline-offset: 3px; word-break: break-all; }

    @media (max-width: 460px) {
      .author-card { justify-content: center; text-align: center; }
      .author-text { text-align: left; }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
